    "result_text": "All findings addressed. Summary of what I changed and why.\n\n## Findings 2 & 3 \u2014 service structure (agreed, fixed)\n`applyMembershipLevelCap` (`ClinicServiceImpl.java`) no longer open-codes a fourth copy of the household-counting loop or stamp scratch state ad-hoc. It now:\n- selects the existing, non-deleted members sharing the `householdId`, then\n- delegates size-stamping to the existing `populateHouseholdSizes(members)` helper (its designated purpose), and\n- derives `maxLevel` from the stamped members.\n\nThis removes the duplicated counting logic and the inline `member.setHouseholdSize(...)` scratch mutation \u2014 the only entity-size stamping now lives in the two `populateHouseholdSize(s)` helpers.\n\n## Finding 1 \u2014 dead code (agreed; resolved per this codebase's convention)\nThe test requires a second same-household owner to be admitted, so the household-duplicate *rejection* really is gone. I split the cleanup by artifact type:\n\n- **Removed outright** (pure internal code, no serialization/persistence contract, now unreachable): `HouseholdDuplicateException.java`, its `@ExceptionHandler`/method in `ExceptionControllerAdvice`, and the stale import.\n- **Retained + re-documented** the `sharesHousehold` (and adjusted `possibleDuplicate`) fields. These are part of the OpenAPI/DTO contract, and this codebase already establishes the convention of keeping such fields inert with explanatory Javadoc (`possibleDuplicate` is explicitly *\"Retained as part of the API/persistence contract but always false\"*). I rewrote the now-stale Javadoc and the two OpenAPI descriptions to state the household-duplicate 409 rule has been superseded by the membership-level cap, so `sharesHousehold` is now inert. This keeps the generated DTOs stable (no regeneration/MapStruct risk) while eliminating the misleading docs the review flagged.\n\nOn the \"overloaded method identity\" phrasing: `applyMembershipLevelCap` is its own well-named method \u2014 it didn't inherit `assertHouseholdUnique`'s identity, only its call-site position inside `saveOwner`'s `creating` block, which is the natural place for it. The genuine problem there was the dead rule left behind, now cleaned up.\n\n## Verification\nFull suite: `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0` \u2014 **BUILD SUCCESS**. No remaining references to the removed exception; the only `setHouseholdSize` calls are inside the `populateHouseholdSize(s)` helpers.",
