    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: CHANGES REQUESTED\n\nThe `getMembershipLevel`/`computeMembershipLevel` split in `Owner` is clean and idiomatic, and the persisted `membership_level_cap` column (with schema updates across all four dialects) is a reasonable way to \"capture at creation time.\" But the service-layer change has real structural problems.\n\n**1. The change repurposes `assertHouseholdUnique` instead of being additive, and leaves a feature's worth of dead code behind.**\n`applyMembershipLevelCap` occupies the old `assertHouseholdUnique` slot and its call site (`ClinicServiceImpl.java:351`). Nothing now throws `HouseholdDuplicateException` and nothing reads the `sharesHousehold` flag \u2014 I grepped `src/main/java` and the only remaining references are declarations/handlers, not users:\n- `HouseholdDuplicateException` (the class) and its `@ExceptionHandler` in `ExceptionControllerAdvice` (lines 155\u2013165) are now unreachable.\n- `Owner.sharesHousehold` (field + getter/setter at `Owner.java:129`, `466\u2013471`) and its Javadoc at `Owner.java:91` describe a rule that no longer exists.\n\nA maintainer would find this jarring: the household-uniqueness rule silently vanished, leaving an orphaned exception, HTTP-409 handler, and transient field with stale docs. If dropping that rule is intended, the dead exception/handler/field/Javadoc should be removed in the same change; if not, capping should be added alongside the check, not on top of its corpse. Either way, don't overload one method's identity to do it.\n\n**2. `applyMembershipLevelCap` mutates sibling entities as scratch state \u2014 logic in the wrong unit.**\nLines 662\u2013664 call `member.setHouseholdSize(householdCount)` on *other* managed `Owner` instances pulled from `ownerRepository.findAll()`, purely so `member.getMembershipLevel()` computes against the right household size. Using a shared transient field as temporary scratch on entities that aren't the subject of this method is a hack, and doing it to managed entities inside a `@Transactional` method is the kind of side effect a reviewer flags. Deriving each member's level should not require stamping state onto the member.\n\n**3. Household counting is now duplicated a third time.**\nThe `for (existing : findAll()) { if householdId.equals(...) count++ }` pattern already lives in `populateHouseholdSize` (675) and `populateHouseholdSizes` (699); `applyMembershipLevelCap` reimplements it inline (650\u2013657). Worse, `saveOwner` runs `applyMembershipLevelCap` (its own `findAll`) and then immediately `populateHouseholdSize(owner)` (354, another `findAll`) \u2014 two full-table scans for overlapping work. Reuse the existing household-size stamping (e.g. populate members' sizes via the existing helper) and derive `maxLevel` from that, rather than open-coding a fourth variant of the same loop.",
